      "role": "user",
      "content": "I understand the comparison, but I'm still not seeing how that solves the core issue of the \"disruptive agency\" I mentioned. You said LCC provides more specificity, which is good in theory, but doesn't that actually make the problem worse in practice? \n\nIf an item is moved or \"drifted\" into a completely wrong section, a more complex, multi-part alphanumeric code in LCC makes it much harder to find through a simple shelf scan compared to a short Dewey number. If these items are behaving in a way that seems to intentionally defy the registry, isn't LCC basically creating a more complex hiding place for them? \n\nHow can a more granular system provide \"integrity\" if the sheer complexity of the classification makes the physical discovery of misplaced items significantly more difficult? I'm looking for the actual mechanics of how these systems handle high-entropy environments where the physical location of an object is constantly at odds with its assigned metadata.",
